    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d732">

                <pb facs="1006" n="994" sameAs="frus:frus1945v06/pg994"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.30/10–1845: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-10-18T20:00:00+03:00">October 18, 1945—8
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 18—2:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3594. ReDept 2175, October 17. I took the occasion of my call on Molotov today to
                    hand him your personal message regarding sinking of the Japanese Fleet.</p>
                <p>Molotov, after reading your message, stated that he would have to study it and
                    consult with his naval authorities before making a reply. He inquired whether it
                    was a question of sinking the entire fleet, both surface and submarine. I told
                    him that I had no further information other than that contained in your message,
                    which I interpreted to indicate the entire fleet. He asked whether I had any
                    information as to the number, character and condition of the Japanese Fleet in
                    addition to what I had given Stalin<anchor n="47" xml:id="d732-f1"/> in August.
                    He referred to an estimate which the Navy Dept had sent me on August 31 for
                    transmission to Stalin at his request. I explained that I had no further
                    information <pb facs="1007" n="995" xml:id="pg995"/> but that his naval
                    authorities might have received later information through the Soviet
                    representatives in Japan.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d732-f1" n="47" xml:id="d732-f1-content">
                <p>Marshal Iosif Vissarionovich Stalin, Chairman of the Council of Commissars
                    (Premier) of the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
